Maintainer asked for a design 2026-11-03, following the k29 research (option c): the hub loads the class itself so tools/list needs no process; calls go to a worker forked from the hub (no exec), spoken to through the existing stdio client, idle-stopped. Measured in k29: ~6 MB own memory per running wiki vs ~55 MB for a stdio mcp-wiki, ready in 11 ms, and no loop stall (in-process history call stalled an unrelated upstream 5.1 s). Bends the 'one daemon process' hazard — the design must lay that out for the maintainer's decision. Deliverable: a design draft under docs/superpowers/specs/, no implementation.


2026-11-03 Design draft written and staged (not committed): docs/superpowers/specs/2026-11-03-forked-class-workers-design.md. It covers the mechanism (Upstream::Fork as a 4-override Upstream::Stdio subclass, hub.fork knob, in-process manifest with no cache or hash), the fork-safety recipe with prototype evidence, the hazards one by one, memory measured as ΔPSS/USS, the binary, tests, and 6 open questions for the maintainer. Q1 is whether forked workers are acceptable under a reworded one-process rule; the draft assumes no answer.
Measured (scratchpad prototype in a real hub under EV and Poll, plus a recipe-off control): a running forked mcp-wiki costs ΔPSS +16–18 MB over hub+worker, plus +5.9 MB for the class loaded in the hub; stdio mcp-wiki costs ~52 MB PSS. Ready in 21–35 ms vs 1.5–3.6 s. Fork stall 2–6 ms. No loop stall: lag ≤41 ms vs 1.6–2.2 s for an in-process history call. This corrects k29's "~6 MB per wiki", which was Private_Dirty of the worker only.
Drift, for the p5-mcp-hub board: a `class` entry silently accepts and ignores env, cwd, headers, type and hub.idle_timeout/request_timeout/always_on. Also: Upstream::Stdio::stop sends TERM together with EOF, which can kill a child mid git commit (index.lock left behind); this applies to stdio too.
Proposed cards are listed in the draft (6 for p5-mcp-hub, 1 plus a k62/k63 priority note for p5-mcp-wiki). None were created.

